## Required conceptual model

Keep these representations separate:

```text
tracer samples
→ accurate track-centre spline model
→ routing graph derived from splines
→ controller localization and physics
```

The spline model is the geometric source of truth. The graph is only a routing abstraction.

The tracer uses a compact locomotive. Its position must be converted into the most accurate possible track-centre representation using the stock geometry, bogey offsets, IR movement semantics, successive samples, and an explicit residual bound.

The front coupler is not the tracer reference. It is used only for stopping.

The stopping target is the front coupler position. Do not treat raw `getPos()` as the F3 hitbox or automatically as the exact rail centreline.

## Spline and graph requirements

Represent track sections as 3D cubic Bézier splines with:

- control points;
- both traversal orientations;
- arc-length representation;
- tangent, grade, and curvature;
- fitting error;
- source trace metadata;
- stable identifiers.

Straight segments are degenerate Bézier curves. IR turns must not be described as exact circles unless the bytecode justifies that claim.

Support repeated trace runs. New traces must be matched against existing splines and may extend, continue, split, overlap, intersect, or create new spline geometry.

Use 3D distance, tangent, curvature, and arc-length matching. Do not merge paths merely because their 2D projections intersect.

Automatically distinguish wherever possible:

- continuation;
- extension;
- genuine shared connection;
- isolated same-level crossing;
- height-separated overpass;
- unresolved ambiguity.

A crossing preserves separate spline paths and does not create a shared physical connection. An overpass is separate if its 3D distance exceeds the certified tolerance.

Manual classification is allowed only when observations cannot determine topology, such as an unexplored branch or coincident same-level paths with indistinguishable connectivity. Ambiguous cases must remain unresolved and unavailable for routing; never silently guess.

Build the routing graph from validated spline identifiers and arc-length intervals. Normally physical splines produce symmetric bidirectional graph edges.

Stations and signals:

- map to the closest spline only within an explicit tolerance;
- store spline ID, arc-length coordinate, and permitted approach orientation;
- impose direction constraints on the associated graph edge or interval.

A station or signal has one configured approach direction. Do not claim that the opposite approach is valid unless separately configured.

The train validates only the splines, vertices, and edges of its active route. It must not scan the entire graph. If an active route section changes, invalidate the route and invoke normal route finding against the latest graph. If no route exists, report the problem and stop.

